# Adaptation: guide maintenance

Use for user-facing knowledge/translation procedure. Default output
`docs/instructions/guides.md`; copy payload only, fill fields, recalculate links.
[Metadata](../../references/metadata.md) owns revision fields/types; use the
[user guide](../guides/user-guide.md.tmpl) and [glossary](../guides/glossary.md.tmpl).
All procedural responsibilities are essential. Omit a screenshot or translation
only if absent/not required in the actual scope; do not invent one for the template.

## Payload

````markdown
---
id: "{{project_id}}-INSTRUCTION-GUIDES"
title: "User guides and translations"
kind: instruction
status: draft
created: "{{created_iso_date}}"
updated: "{{updated_iso_date}}"
areas: [documentation, guides]
summary: "User scenarios and implemented interfaces become usable, versioned guides with honest language coverage."
read_when: "A user capability, command, screen or limitation changes, or release guides/translations are prepared."
---

# Guides and translations

## Scope

Primary language: {{primary_language_from_profile}}. Required release translations:
{{release_languages_or_explicit_none}}. Existing primary sources remain authoritative;
not all internal docs need translation. This procedure authorizes no product runs,
private screenshots, paid tools, publication or release.

## Write from the real task

1. Find the scenario, implemented interface/contract and scoped evidence at
   {{scenario_contract_and_interface_routes}}. Separate source inspection, real
   executed path and future proposal. Never turn a mockup into a shipped how-to.
2. Update the existing guide at {{guide_entry_and_locations}}. A new standalone
   task may use {{guide_form_route}}. Getting started, task steps, reference and
   troubleshooting can be sections, not four mandatory files.
3. Explain result, supported version/platform, prerequisites, exact actions, observable
   success, important failure/next action and limitations. Keep technical commands,
   flags, keys, IDs and units unchanged. Link precise reference data instead of
   duplicating its authoritative table. Avoid agent report language in user steps.

## Language revision workflow

Give each language its own ID and `language`, reciprocal ordinary Markdown links
and `related` IDs. Primary: `source_revision`. Translation: `translation_source`
(primary ID), `translation_source_revision` (actually checked edition),
`translation_state` (current/outdated/unknown). Use the shared metadata schema.

Content change increments the primary edition. Compare each translation's checked
edition to the primary: unequal means outdated, absent/inaccessible means unknown.
Equal revisions require substantive review, not date equality. Update affected steps,
restrictions, examples and image labels before advancing the checked edition/state.
Pure formatting can justify no translation impact without an artificial revision bump.

The feature/guide author updates affected knowledge; {{guide_owner_role}} maintains
pairs and one glossary at {{glossary_route_or_existing_term_owner}}. The release owner
{{release_owner_role}} assigns actual translation work and confirms required languages
before that release. A deferred version keeps its old checked edition and a visible
warning/current-primary link; do not call it current or release-ready. Missing required
work outside scope goes to its exact owner, not an invisible waiver.

## Illustrations and verification

Use real safe screenshots when they explain a step and are available/authorized.
Version/language must match or carry an explicit explanation; alt/caption and origin
remain. Preserve original evidence when deriving a crop. A future concept is labeled
as such; Figma/image generation never block a useful text guide.

Before freeze inspect source fidelity, important failure, both language links, unique
IDs, revision/state consistency, technical tokens and image provenance. Run
{{verified_docs_check_or_unconfigured}}; report actual exits and unrun product/visual
checks separately. Static checks cannot judge translation quality or installed readiness.
Add the nearest entry and follow the project's acceptance/save policy; retain required
independence without appointing another reviewer. Stop after the selected task.

## Update conditions

Revisit when interface steps, guide locations, primary language, release languages,
revision schema or responsible roles change. Policy changes require their actual owner.
````
